From: AxionOS <noreply@axionos>
Subject: [PATCH] UserManagerService: run the dual apps user-removed hook outside mUsersLock and catch failures

removeUserState() runs on a bare `new Thread(...)` from the USER_REMOVED
receiver. It called AxDualAppsService.onUserRemoved() inside
synchronized (mUsersLock); that hook calls AppOpsService.setMode() for
every dual app (resetAllPackageOps), so an exception there escaped the
thread and killed system_server, and the app-ops call ran under the
user-list lock.

Fix: call the hook after leaving the mUsersLock block and catch
RuntimeException, logging it instead of crashing.
---
diff --git a/services/core/java/com/android/server/pm/UserManagerService.java b/services/core/java/com/android/server/pm/UserManagerService.java
index 09bd978..e54abce 100644
--- a/services/core/java/com/android/server/pm/UserManagerService.java
+++ b/services/core/java/com/android/server/pm/UserManagerService.java
@@ -7439,9 +7439,15 @@ public class UserManagerService extends IUserManager.Stub {
         synchronized (mUsersLock) {
             removeUserDataLU(userId);
             getActivityManagerInternal().onUserRemoved(userId);
+        }
+        // Runs on a bare Thread (see the USER_REMOVED receiver): an escaping exception here
+        // kills system_server, and app-ops calls must not be made under mUsersLock.
+        try {
             if (AxDualAppsService.get() != null) {
                 AxDualAppsService.get().onUserRemoved(userId);
             }
+        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
+            Slog.e(LOG_TAG, "Dual apps cleanup failed for removed user " + userId, e);
         }
         synchronized (mUserStates) {
             mUserStates.delete(userId);
